{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-8720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $5\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-5\\sqrt{3}=0$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f6d16574ff9c8d0594167fd1e17f037790bc140e7ce680441c50fc6287d6fa"} {"question" "q-8720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-5\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 5\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =5\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=-\\pi +{x}_{1}=-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f6d16574ff9c8d0594167fd1e17f037790bc140e7ce680441c50fc6287d6fa"} {"question" "q-8720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4d79447af1988d5ced70e51c837ae76b1700da88c0e3927e642fdb2c886db88d"} {"question" "q-8720" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f6d16574ff9c8d0594167fd1e17f037790bc140e7ce680441c50fc6287d6fa"} {"question" "q-8720" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}"] ["math" "-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}"] ["math" "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f6d16574ff9c8d0594167fd1e17f037790bc140e7ce680441c50fc6287d6fa"} {"question" "q-8720" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f6d16574ff9c8d0594167fd1e17f037790bc140e7ce680441c50fc6287d6fa"} {"question" "q-72824" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-8$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f6d16574ff9c8d0594167fd1e17f037790bc140e7ce680441c50fc6287d6fa"} {"question" "q-72824" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-8)=-1.446\\,4…≈-1.446\\,4.\n$$\n\nSince $-1.446\\,4$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\pi -1.446\\,4≈1.695\\,2, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =2\\pi -1.446\\,4≈4.836\\,8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈1.695\\,2\\cdot 4.836\\,8≈8.2\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f6d16574ff9c8d0594167fd1e17f037790bc140e7ce680441c50fc6287d6fa"} {"question" "q-72824" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4d79447af1988d5ced70e51c837ae76b1700da88c0e3927e642fdb2c886db88d"} {"question" "q-72824" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f6d16574ff9c8d0594167fd1e17f037790bc140e7ce680441c50fc6287d6fa"} {"question" "q-72824" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.8"] ["math" "7.8"] ["math" "8.0"] ["math" "8.2"] ["math" "8.4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f6d16574ff9c8d0594167fd1e17f037790bc140e7ce680441c50fc6287d6fa"} {"question" "q-72824" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8.2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f6d16574ff9c8d0594167fd1e17f037790bc140e7ce680441c50fc6287d6fa"} {"question" "q-121542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/assets/56726fc8331106df9b20aed561a2ed3b93fe12d90da85a8d1b92e610b19033f7.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the line $y=\\sqrt{3}.$ Find the $x$-coordinate of the point $B.$\n\n$$\n\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f6d16574ff9c8d0594167fd1e17f037790bc140e7ce680441c50fc6287d6fa"} {"question" "q-121542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two equations gives: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside $-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<x<-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ To get a value that's inside the required domain, we add $-\\pi $  to the principal value. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=-\\pi +x=-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of $B$ is $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f6d16574ff9c8d0594167fd1e17f037790bc140e7ce680441c50fc6287d6fa"} {"question" "q-121542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4d79447af1988d5ced70e51c837ae76b1700da88c0e3927e642fdb2c886db88d"} {"question" "q-121542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f6d16574ff9c8d0594167fd1e17f037790bc140e7ce680441c50fc6287d6fa"} {"question" "q-121542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}"] ["math" "-\\frac{6\\pi }{5}"] ["math" "-\\frac{8\\pi }{7}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f6d16574ff9c8d0594167fd1e17f037790bc140e7ce680441c50fc6287d6fa"} {"question" "q-121542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f6d16574ff9c8d0594167fd1e17f037790bc140e7ce680441c50fc6287d6fa"} {"question" "q-274852" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/assets/047f02622fc176fee8c6cb0e81a0434ee208648dca14102b1d96e37b901d51f2.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the line $y=1.$\n\nThe **exact** value of the $x$-coordinate of the point $A$ is \n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f6d16574ff9c8d0594167fd1e17f037790bc140e7ce680441c50fc6287d6fa"} {"question" "q-274852" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two equations gives:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=1\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside $-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<x<-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ To get a value that's inside the required domain, we add $-\\pi $ to the principal value. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =x+(-\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f6d16574ff9c8d0594167fd1e17f037790bc140e7ce680441c50fc6287d6fa"} {"question" "q-274852" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4d79447af1988d5ced70e51c837ae76b1700da88c0e3927e642fdb2c886db88d"} {"question" "q-274852" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f6d16574ff9c8d0594167fd1e17f037790bc140e7ce680441c50fc6287d6fa"} {"question" "q-274852" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3\\pi}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f6d16574ff9c8d0594167fd1e17f037790bc140e7ce680441c50fc6287d6fa"} {"question" "q-321622" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the solutions **in degrees** to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{6}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ Round your answers to **the nearest integer**.\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f6d16574ff9c8d0594167fd1e17f037790bc140e7ce680441c50fc6287d6fa"} {"question" "q-321622" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{6})=9.4623{…}^{∘}≈{9}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${9}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={9}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={180}^{∘}+9.4623{…}^{∘}≈{189}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order and rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions are $x={9}^{∘}$ and $x={189}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f6d16574ff9c8d0594167fd1e17f037790bc140e7ce680441c50fc6287d6fa"} {"question" "q-321622" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4d79447af1988d5ced70e51c837ae76b1700da88c0e3927e642fdb2c886db88d"} {"question" "q-321622" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f6d16574ff9c8d0594167fd1e17f037790bc140e7ce680441c50fc6287d6fa"} {"question" "q-321622" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f6d16574ff9c8d0594167fd1e17f037790bc140e7ce680441c50fc6287d6fa"} {"question" "q-321622" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f6d16574ff9c8d0594167fd1e17f037790bc140e7ce680441c50fc6287d6fa"} {"question" "q-321622" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "189"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977868/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f6d16574ff9c8d0594167fd1e1